1
00:00:01,700 --> 00:00:04,104
It's 8:55 on a Monday.

2
00:00:04,354 --> 00:00:08,274
Your inbox is full, your calendar has
three clashes,

3
00:00:08,294 --> 00:00:13,999
and someone wants last quarter's number
checked against the CRM before nine.

4
00:00:14,249 --> 00:00:15,982
None of that is hard.

5
00:00:16,232 --> 00:00:19,662
It's just busywork spread across five
different apps.

6
00:00:19,912 --> 00:00:27,267
What if you could hand it over in plain
language, watch it get done, and stay in
charge the whole time?

7
00:00:27,517 --> 00:00:35,656
That's UiPath Delegate: a digital sidekick
that learns from you and does real work
across your apps.

8
00:00:35,906 --> 00:00:41,689
In the next ten minutes I'll show you what
it does, how it works, and how you keep
control.

9
00:00:43,239 --> 00:00:47,878
Delegate is a general-purpose agent with
computer use.

10
00:00:48,128 --> 00:00:50,799
You ask for something in plain language,

11
00:00:50,819 --> 00:00:55,476
and it works across your browser and your
applications to get it done.

12
00:00:55,726 --> 00:01:03,164
You can start four ways: type a prompt,
speak a command, record your screen, or
start from a template.

13
00:01:03,414 --> 00:01:05,910
Four ideas sit behind it.

14
00:01:06,160 --> 00:01:12,696
First, offload the busywork: the
repetitive admin that drains your time and
focus.

15
00:01:12,945 --> 00:01:19,942
Second, done, not suggested: it goes
beyond advice and completes the task end
to end.

16
00:01:20,193 --> 00:01:24,563
Third, it's trainable, with skills that
grow with how you work.

17
00:01:24,813 --> 00:01:32,481
And fourth, it's enterprise-ready:
programmatic execution, built to be
reliable and secure.

18
00:01:32,731 --> 00:01:39,057
AI can remove busywork, but only if it
knows how to work inside the enterprise.

19
00:01:39,308 --> 00:01:42,004
That's the gap Delegate is built for.

20
00:01:43,554 --> 00:01:48,578
Let me show you the loop, because it's
simple and it's the same every time.

21
00:01:48,828 --> 00:01:52,335
One: you ask. In plain language, in a
session.

22
00:01:52,585 --> 00:01:54,888
No code, no flowchart.

23
00:01:55,138 --> 00:01:57,138
Two: Delegate plans.

24
00:01:57,388 --> 00:02:04,017
It works out the steps and which tools it
needs, breaking a complex workflow into
pieces.

25
00:02:04,266 --> 00:02:06,755
Three: it acts, and it shows you.

26
00:02:07,006 --> 00:02:10,023
Every action is visible as it happens.

27
00:02:10,272 --> 00:02:16,175
If it's heading the wrong way, you can
interrupt, correct, or redirect at any
point.

28
00:02:16,425 --> 00:02:20,234
Delegate also learns from your feedback
over time.

29
00:02:20,484 --> 00:02:22,715
Four: it stops when it should.

30
00:02:22,965 --> 00:02:31,333
Anything that changes data, sends a
message, or reaches into a sensitive
system waits for your confirmation,

31
00:02:31,353 --> 00:02:36,516
depending on your execution mode and what
your organization allows.

32
00:02:36,767 --> 00:02:42,240
I'll come back to that, because it's the
part that makes this safe to use at work.

33
00:02:42,489 --> 00:02:48,180
Under the hood it handles UI automation,
files, and API calls,

34
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:56,918
and you can attach your own SOPs and
product design documents so it works from
your context, not a guess.

35
00:02:58,468 --> 00:03:00,190
Now let's see it work.

36
00:03:00,440 --> 00:03:05,212
The first thing Delegate is good at is
tasks that cross systems.

37
00:03:05,463 --> 00:03:11,911
Here I ask it to pull a figure from a
report and check the related CRM record.

38
00:03:12,161 --> 00:03:14,565
It doesn't stop after step one.

39
00:03:14,815 --> 00:03:22,286
It handles the whole chain, so I get one
complete answer without piecing the story
together myself.

40
00:03:22,535 --> 00:03:24,593
Next, the morning routine.

41
00:03:24,843 --> 00:03:29,147
I ask it to triage my email and calendar
first thing.

42
00:03:29,397 --> 00:03:33,655
It reads both, and tells me what needs me
and what can wait.

43
00:03:33,905 --> 00:03:38,889
And once the rest is done, I ask it to
draft a follow-up message.

44
00:03:39,139 --> 00:03:42,106
It writes the draft using what it just
found.

45
00:03:42,356 --> 00:03:47,611
I review it, adjust the tone, and only
then does anything get sent.

46
00:03:49,161 --> 00:03:51,864
This pattern shows up across roles.

47
00:03:52,114 --> 00:03:57,988
In our support example, a Product Support
Engineer starts the day with one prompt:

48
00:03:58,008 --> 00:04:02,976
pull my case backlog from Salesforce and
prioritize the day.

49
00:04:03,226 --> 00:04:10,861
Later they summarize the case in front of
them, look up licensing data, and draft a
first response,

50
00:04:10,881 --> 00:04:15,710
all from one conversational surface,
without switching tools.

51
00:04:17,260 --> 00:04:20,342
Some work can't be automated the usual
way.

52
00:04:20,593 --> 00:04:28,650
The system has no usable API, and building
a full automation would cost more than the
task is worth.

53
00:04:28,900 --> 00:04:32,014
For that, you teach Delegate by showing
it.

54
00:04:32,264 --> 00:04:35,030
Record yourself doing the task once.

55
00:04:35,280 --> 00:04:42,886
Delegate turns that recording into a
routine, or a knowledge skill, that it can
repeat on your behalf.

56
00:04:43,137 --> 00:04:50,738
That's the idea behind a trainable
sidekick: you capture your expertise as a
reusable playbook,

57
00:04:50,758 --> 00:04:55,304
document a workflow once, and invoke it
whenever you need it.

58
00:04:55,554 --> 00:04:58,333
And skills aren't stuck with one person.

59
00:04:58,583 --> 00:05:03,112
You can share them across the team, so
everyone works the same way,

60
00:05:03,132 --> 00:05:07,198
and nobody rebuilds what a colleague
already taught it.

61
00:05:08,748 --> 00:05:14,439
Here's where the hours disappear: turning
raw data into something you can send.

62
00:05:14,689 --> 00:05:20,495
Point Delegate at a spreadsheet, a
database export, or a folder of files,

63
00:05:20,515 --> 00:05:24,882
and ask for a presentation, a proposal, or
a report.

64
00:05:25,133 --> 00:05:30,242
It pulls the relevant data, structures it,
and applies your formatting.

65
00:05:30,491 --> 00:05:32,996
What used to take hours takes minutes,

66
00:05:33,016 --> 00:05:37,849
and you're editing a finished draft
instead of staring at a blank slide.

67
00:05:38,099 --> 00:05:42,675
The same idea powers some of the bigger
enterprise use cases:

68
00:05:42,695 --> 00:05:49,289
generating process and solution design
documents, running application test cases,

69
00:05:49,309 --> 00:05:53,804
and taking intake and triage off a
clerical team's plate.

70
00:05:55,354 --> 00:06:03,632
Chat is great, but sometimes you need the
real thing, a proper form or a table you
can actually work in.

71
00:06:03,882 --> 00:06:08,727
When Delegate connects to a system that
provides its own interface,

72
00:06:08,747 --> 00:06:13,775
it can show that system's real screens
inside the conversation.

73
00:06:14,025 --> 00:06:20,849
Lists, forms, tables, and dashboards
render inline, not as a description of
them.

74
00:06:21,099 --> 00:06:25,012
Forms arrive already filled in with
sensible values,

75
00:06:25,032 --> 00:06:30,612
so you change only what matters instead of
describing every field in chat.

76
00:06:30,862 --> 00:06:36,400
You keep the precision of the real
interface without leaving the
conversation.

77
00:06:36,650 --> 00:06:39,218
This works through MCP Apps.

78
00:06:40,768 --> 00:06:44,484
Everything I've shown you so far has one
more trick.

79
00:06:44,734 --> 00:06:48,294
When a session goes well, you don't have
to do it again.

80
00:06:48,544 --> 00:06:53,952
Save any successful Delegate session as a
reusable routine.

81
00:06:54,202 --> 00:07:01,201
Build it once, say a weekly sales pipeline
report, or a monthly expense
categorization,

82
00:07:01,221 --> 00:07:08,578
and from then on you can run it on demand
with a single click, or schedule it to run
automatically.

83
00:07:08,829 --> 00:07:15,994
You invest the time once to teach Delegate
a workflow, then you benefit every time
after that.

84
00:07:16,244 --> 00:07:18,101
And it isn't just for you.

85
00:07:18,351 --> 00:07:25,318
You can share a routine with your team, so
each person isn't rebuilding the same
thing from scratch.

86
00:07:26,867 --> 00:07:31,987
Of course, the question everyone asks is:
how much do I trust it?

87
00:07:32,237 --> 00:07:35,178
You decide, with three approval modes.

88
00:07:35,428 --> 00:07:38,420
Cautious asks before every operation.

89
00:07:38,670 --> 00:07:42,106
It's the safest, with the most approval
prompts.

90
00:07:42,356 --> 00:07:48,438
Adaptive auto-approves known safe
operations, and it's the recommended
setting.

91
00:07:48,689 --> 00:07:55,437
Full access has no approval prompts at
all, so keep it for trusted environments
only.

92
00:07:55,687 --> 00:07:59,668
Inside Adaptive there's a Smart approvals
toggle.

93
00:07:59,918 --> 00:08:05,364
Turn it on and Delegate also approves what
the model judges safe.

94
00:08:05,614 --> 00:08:12,612
You get the fewest prompts, but the model
can misjudge, so that's a trade-off you
choose on purpose.

95
00:08:14,161 --> 00:08:21,903
You can also set allow and deny lists for
apps and URLs, and granular permissions by
task type.

96
00:08:22,153 --> 00:08:27,657
Every action lands in an audit trail, and
your data stays in your control.

97
00:08:29,207 --> 00:08:37,164
Because Delegate ships on the UiPath
platform, every operation inherits
controls you already run:

98
00:08:37,414 --> 00:08:40,999
Orchestrator for scheduling and run
management,

99
00:08:41,019 --> 00:08:45,656
a credential vault so secrets are never
exposed to the agent,

100
00:08:45,676 --> 00:08:49,197
the AI Trust Layer for policy and
redaction,

101
00:08:49,217 --> 00:08:53,121
and role-based access mapped to your
organization.

102
00:08:53,370 --> 00:08:55,928
Not bolted on. Built in.

103
00:08:57,478 --> 00:08:58,887
Let's recap.

104
00:08:59,137 --> 00:09:02,859
Delegate completes tasks that cross
applications.

105
00:09:03,109 --> 00:09:06,147
It learns repetitive work from a
recording.

106
00:09:06,397 --> 00:09:09,906
It turns raw data into a finished
deliverable.

107
00:09:10,156 --> 00:09:12,849
It works through a system's own screens.

108
00:09:13,099 --> 00:09:20,234
And it saves what works as routines you
can reuse and share, all under approval
modes you control.

109
00:09:20,484 --> 00:09:23,776
Delegate the busywork. Keep the judgment.

110
00:09:24,026 --> 00:09:29,844
If you're already on UiPath, Delegate
ships as an extension of what you have.

111
00:09:30,094 --> 00:09:38,088
The UiPath Assistant upgrades into it,
with no new IT approval and no new upgrade
process.

112
00:09:38,338 --> 00:09:42,859
The getting-started guide and the product
page are linked below.

113
00:09:43,109 --> 00:09:48,719
Open a session, ask for one small thing,
and meet your first AI-intern.
